\subsection{Failure Mode Analysis}
\label{sec:failure-modes}

Per-task DAF values reveal a bimodal structure: 5 of 11 task types are \emph{death-dominated} (DAF $\geq 0.95$: \texttt{inclined-plane}, \texttt{freeze}, \texttt{meas-melt-point}, \texttt{id-life-stages-2}, \texttt{grow-fruit}), where virtually all routing signal derives from death asymmetry, while the remaining 6 are \emph{genuine-deterministic} ($0 < \text{DAF} < 0.95$), exhibiting non-trivial non-death score differences alongside death effects. Notably, \texttt{find-non-living-thing} (DAF $= 0$) falls entirely in the latter category.

All 129 death events across both strategies are triggered by a single action type: \texttt{focus on <location>}. This universality is task-independent. Among internal-strategy deaths, 68\% are preceded by \emph{exploration loops}---the agent cycles between locations without task progress before issuing a lethal focus action. The top lethal locations are \texttt{workshop} (21 deaths), \texttt{inventory} (15), and \texttt{kitchen} (11). The deterministic strategy avoids these loops because its constrained action space excludes invalid navigation. All deaths occur at the terminal step of their respective episodes, which is a structural property of ScienceWorld: a lethal action immediately ends the episode. When the internal strategy dies but deterministic survives (70 episodes, 28.6\% of total), the internal agent completes only 65\% as many steps (mean 19.4 vs.\ 30).

